#version 450
#extension GL_EXT_integer_dot_product : require
#extension GL_EXT_control_flow_attributes : enable

// y = W * X for one Q4_0 matrix against a batch of Q8_0 activations.
//
// shaders/matvec.comp is the same product for one column, and for a token that
// is the right shape: there is nothing to share, and the weights are read at
// the card's memory ceiling. It is the wrong shape for a prompt. Giving it
// eight columns instead of one takes the weight traffic down eightfold and
// leaves the kernel reading the activation fourteen times for every once it
// reads the weights — measured, that holds an eight-column pass at 144
// gigabytes a second where a four-column pass reaches 306.
//
// The number that governs it is how much arithmetic a thread does for each
// load it issues. One row by eight columns is eight products against nine
// loads. This kernel gives a thread TM rows by TN columns: TM*TN*8 products
// against TM*5 + TN*10 loads, which at four by four is two and a half times
// better and at eight by four is three and a half. llama.cpp's own Vulkan
// backend draws the same line — its mat-vec kernel stops at eight columns and
// a tiled product takes over above that, and on this architecture its mat-vec
// carries one row per thread exactly as ours does.
//
// So: a workgroup owns BM rows by COLUMNS columns of the answer. It walks the
// shared dimension in chunks of KSTEP blocks, staging both operands in shared
// memory, and each thread keeps its own TM by TN tile of the result in
// registers for the whole walk. Nothing is reduced across threads at the end,
// which is the other thing the mat-vec kernel spends: there, eight lanes split
// one row and fold their partial sums through shared memory.
//
// The weights are in the layout vk/mixture.go uploads: a row is all of its
// fp16 scales, then all of its nibbles. The activation is one column after
// another, its magnitudes then its scales then its corrections — the same
// buffers shaders/matvec.comp reads, so the two kernels could share a
// descriptor set and the caller pick between them by the width of the pass.
//
// **Nothing binds this yet, and here is what it measures.** On the Qwen3 4B's
// ffn_down, thirty-two columns a pass: 5.9 microseconds a column, against the
// eight-column mat-vec's 14 and the one-column mat-vec's 38. ffn_gate is 8.3.
// It got there in two steps, both of which are in the file now:
//
//   - **The operands are read sixteen bytes at a time.** The staging loops
//     used to issue one scalar load per uint, and that was the kernel's whole
//     cost: aliasing the bindings with uvec4 blocks took ffn_down from 9.4
//     microseconds a column to 7.0 and ffn_gate from 10.0 to 8.5, at an
//     unchanged tile. llama.cpp reads its own operands the same width.
//   - **The tile was then swept again**, because a kernel whose loads dominate
//     answers a sweep differently from one whose arithmetic does. The answer
//     moved from two rows by two columns over sixty-four rows and eight blocks
//     to **one by two over thirty-two rows and sixteen blocks**: fewer rows to
//     a workgroup, so twice as many workgroups, and a walk twice as long
//     between the barriers. That is another 7.0 to 5.9.
//
// **The shared dimension is then split.** A workgroup writes BM rows, so a
// matrix with few rows gives few workgroups: ffn_down gives eighty against
// sixty-four compute units that hold two of these each, and the card runs out
// of anything to schedule. Cutting the shared dimension into p.split slices,
// one workgroup each, multiplies them, and shaders/matmul_reduce.comp adds the
// slices afterwards. On ffn_down at thirty-two columns that is 4.24
// microseconds a column against 3.59, and in the engine the down projection of
// a block goes from 6.36 milliseconds to 4.40 over thirty-six blocks. On
// ffn_gate, which has three hundred and four workgroups already, the same
// split is a fifth *slower* — the reduction is not free and there was nothing
// to gain. vk/matmul.go's matmulSplit is where the row count decides.
//
// What was ruled out on the way, so that it is not ruled out again:
//
//   - **Bank conflicts were real and are fixed.** A column of the activation
//     was KSTEP*8 uints, which at KSTEP four is thirty-two exactly — the bank
//     count — so every thread of a wave read one bank. Padding the strides by
//     one uint was worth 1.5x, from 24 to 36 gigabytes a second.
//   - **A bigger tile has never once won here.** Two by two beat four by four
//     before the wide loads and after them, and eight by four — llama.cpp's
//     own shape — is four times slower than one by two at every size measured.
//     On this card the product is not short of arithmetic to issue, it is
//     short of work in flight, and every row a thread takes on costs threads.
//   - **Removing half the kernel measures nothing.** Compiled without the
//     staging, the compute reads a loop-invariant shared array and the whole
//     walk is hoisted out; compiled without the compute, the staging is dead
//     and removed. Both report four hundred gigabytes a second and both are
//     lies.
//   - **BM and vk/matmul.go's matmulRows are one number in two places.** Left
//     disagreeing, the dispatch covers a fraction of the rows, the answer is
//     zero below it, and the benchmark reports the fraction as a speed-up.
//   - **Reading the staged operands sixteen bytes at a time**, one
//     ds_read_b128 where there are four and eight scalar reads, which is the
//     obvious way to cut a third of the inner loop's instructions. It is 1.4
//     times slower, at every tile and both widths, and it stays 1.4 times
//     slower with the activation's block index XOR-swizzled so that sixteen
//     lanes on sixteen columns land on sixteen different bank quads. Wide
//     shared reads are not what this card wants; the scalar loop at an odd
//     stride is.
//
// **The pass is then widened, and the columns blocked.** The cost of a pass is
// a straight line: fifty-five microseconds fixed, which is the twelve
// megabytes of ffn_down at two hundred and twenty-five gigabytes a second,
// plus 3.6 microseconds a column. Widening only divides the fixed half further
// and the return falls away quickly — 3.58 microseconds a column at
// thirty-two, 3.36 at sixty-four, 3.32 at a hundred and twenty-eight on
// ffn_down; 6.56, 6.03 and 5.76 on ffn_gate. In the engine that is a Qwen3 4B
// prompt of 1521 tokens a second against 1625.
//
// A wide pass cannot give a workgroup the whole batch: the activation staged
// for a step is COLUMNS*(KSTEP*8+1) uints, which at sixty-four columns is
// already more shared memory than a workgroup may have. So BN columns to a
// workgroup and a grid of rows over BM by columns over BN, which is the shape
// llama.cpp's own product has always had and the one thing this kernel was
// missing. **The dispatch has to count those column groups**, and left
// uncounted the kernel answers a fraction of the batch and leaves the rest
// zero — the same trap BM and matmulRows are, one matrix further on, and it
// measured as a speed-up until a parity test at a width above BN caught it.
//
// **Built with BYID this is the same product read by expert**, which is what a
// mixture's down projection wants for a prompt.
//
// That projection's first shape was a kernel of its own, a mat-vec with a few
// columns bolted on: one output row to a team of lanes, no shared staging, no
// register tile. It ran the 26B's expert stack at about ninety gigabytes a
// second and nothing parametric moved it — sixteen, thirty-two or sixty-four
// columns per weight read instead of eight were monotonically *worse* (1561,
// 1504, 1384, 1226 tokens a second at two hundred and fifty-six columns) even
// though each halving quarters the weight traffic, and the lane count was flat
// between four and eight. A kernel whose speed does not answer to its traffic
// is not short of bandwidth; it is short of arithmetic per load, which is what
// the tile below is. Brought here the same stage measures **34.4 milliseconds
// against 43.5 over thirty blocks**, a fifth off, and one kernel is gone.
//
// It is a fifth and not a third, and the reason is worth keeping: the whole
// pass moved 243 milliseconds to 240. The mixture's prompt is not spent where
// this rewrite was aimed. Two hundred and fifty-six columns cost 240
// milliseconds and sixty-four cost 70, which is a straight line of about nine
// tenths of a millisecond a column over an intercept of eleven — the stack is
// read once either way, so what the extra columns buy is arithmetic, and it is
// the arithmetic and not the stream that the pass is made of. The gate and up
// half is thirty-six per cent of it and still the old shape.
//
// Read by expert, only three things change and none of them is the walk. A
// workgroup answers BN entries of one expert's list rather than BN columns of
// the batch: the weights start at that expert's slab, the activation column is
// pairs[] rather than the column index, and the answer is written to the
// pair's own row for shaders/moe_id_combine.comp to add. The list is short —
// a hundred and twenty-eight experts share eight choices from each of the
// pass's columns, so an expert holds sixteen of them at two hundred and
// fifty-six columns — and a workgroup grid cannot be built from a count the
// card computes. So shaders/moe_scatter.comp writes a plan beside the counts:
// one entry per (expert, stretch of BN) that has work, and the dispatch is
// that plan's worst case with the entries past its end returning at once.
//
// **KSTEP is eight here and sixteen everywhere else.** An expert's shared
// dimension is seven hundred and four, which is twenty-two blocks: at sixteen
// the walk is two steps of which the second is ten blocks of padding, and that
// padding was the whole of the shape's early loss. At eight it is three steps
// and two blocks of padding — 34.4 milliseconds against 47.7. The tile itself
// was swept again for this shape and did not move: two rows by two columns
// beats one by four, two by four, four by two and four by four, as it does at
// every width above.

#ifndef COLUMNS
#define COLUMNS 32
#endif

// The tile, as preprocessor arithmetic because the workgroup size has to be a
// literal. TM rows by TN columns to a thread, BM rows by COLUMNS to a group.
// Swept rather than reasoned to, twice — see the header. BM must equal
// vk/matmul.go's matmulRows.
#ifndef TM
#define TM     2
#endif
#ifndef TN
#define TN     2
#endif
#ifndef BM
#define BM     32
#endif
#ifndef KSTEP
#define KSTEP  16
#endif
// BN is how many columns of the batch one workgroup answers, which above
// thirty-two is no longer all of them. The activation staged for a step is
// BN*(KSTEP*8+1) uints, and at sixty-four columns and sixteen blocks that is
// thirty-three kilobytes — more shared memory than a workgroup may have. So a
// wide pass is cut across its columns as well as its rows, the way llama.cpp's
// own product is: its grid is rows over BM by columns over BN, and it never
// gave a workgroup the whole batch either.
#ifndef BN
#if COLUMNS > 32
#define BN 32
#else
#define BN COLUMNS
#endif
#endif
#define CGROUPS (COLUMNS / BN)
#define COLTH  (BN / TN)
#define THREADS ((BM / TM) * COLTH)

// How many of each operand one thread fetches per step. The staging is issued
// a whole step ahead into these, so they have to be a fixed count.
#define WLOADS (BM * KSTEP / THREADS)
#define ALOADS (BN * KSTEP * 2 / THREADS)
#define ADLOADS (BN * KSTEP / THREADS)

// The strides of the staged operands, padded by one.
//
// Shared memory is thirty-two banks of four bytes, and two addresses a
// multiple of thirty-two apart are the same bank. A column of the activation
// is KSTEP*8 uints, which at KSTEP four is thirty-two exactly: every thread of
// a wave would read the same bank on every load, sixteen ways at once.
// Measured, that alone held this kernel at 24 gigabytes a second. One uint of
// padding makes the stride odd and spreads the wave over the banks.
#define SA_STRIDE (KSTEP * 4 + 1)
#define SB_STRIDE (KSTEP * 8 + 1)
#define SD_STRIDE (KSTEP + 1)

layout(local_size_x = THREADS) in;

layout(std430, binding = 0) readonly  buffer W  { uint  w[];   };
layout(std430, binding = 1) readonly  buffer AQ { uint  aq[];  };  // the activation, Q8_0

// The same two buffers seen sixteen bytes at a time.
//
// The staging loops below issue one scalar load per uint, which is what the
// kernel spends most of its time on; llama.cpp's own quantised product reads
// its operands a hundred and twenty-eight bits at a time and aliases the
// binding to do it. GLSL allows two blocks on one binding as long as the
// offsets line up, and both of ours do: a Q8_0 block of the activation is
// thirty-two bytes, and a row of weights is eighteen bytes a block, so the
// nibbles are sixteen-byte aligned whenever the block count is a multiple of
// eight. The kernel checks that at run time and keeps the scalar path for the
// shapes where it is not.
layout(std430, binding = 0) readonly  buffer W4  { uvec4 w4[];  };
layout(std430, binding = 1) readonly  buffer AQ4 { uvec4 aq4[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 2) readonly  buffer AS { float as_[]; };  // its scales, then its corrections
layout(std430, binding = 3) writeonly buffer Y  { float y[];   };

#ifdef BYID
// The counts shaders/moe_scatter.comp wrote, and the plan it wrote after them:
// plan[0] is how many entries the plan has, and each entry after it is an
// expert and the first of its BN list entries. A workgroup past the plan's end
// reads one uint and returns.
layout(std430, binding = 4) readonly buffer CN { uint counts[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 5) readonly buffer PR { uint pairs[];  };
layout(std430, binding = 6) readonly buffer PL { uint plan[];   };
#endif

layout(push_constant) uniform Push {
    uint dim;   // outputs
    uint ffn;   // inputs
    uint used;  // one, and only here so that every kernel takes the same block
    uint act;   // the activation, which is spent before this kernel runs
    uint col;   // the gate kernel's, and not read here
    uint split; // slices of the shared dimension, one for the whole of it
    uint cap;   // room in one expert's list, which only BYID reads
} p;

// The staged operands. Ten kilobytes, which leaves a compute unit several
// workgroups.
shared uint  sa[BM * SA_STRIDE];        // the nibbles, four uints a block
shared float sad[BM * SD_STRIDE];       // the weight scales
shared uint  sb[BN * SB_STRIDE];   // the magnitudes, eight uints a block
shared float sbd[BN * SD_STRIDE];  // the activation scales
shared float sbc[BN * SD_STRIDE];  // its corrections, the scale times the sum, eight times over

#ifdef BYID
// This workgroup's BN entries of the list, read once. A stretch that runs past
// the end of the list repeats its first entry rather than reading nothing: the
// answer is dropped at the write, and an entry that exists keeps every load in
// range.
shared uint spair[BN];
shared bool salive[BN];
#define SCBASE(pair) (((pair) / p.used) * 2u * p.used * nb + ((pair) % p.used) * nb)
#define ACOL(c)      spair[(c)]
#define ASCALE(c, b) as_[SCBASE(spair[(c)]) + (b)]
#define ACORR(c, b)  as_[SCBASE(spair[(c)]) + p.used * nb + (b)]
#else
#define ACOL(c)      (col0 + (c))
#define ASCALE(c, b) as_[(col0 + (c)) * 2u * nb + (b)]
#define ACORR(c, b)  as_[(col0 + (c)) * 2u * nb + nb + (b)]
#endif

void main() {
    uint t   = gl_LocalInvocationID.x;
    uint tr  = t / uint(COLTH);            // which TM rows this thread owns
    uint tc  = t % uint(COLTH);            // which TN columns

    uint nb  = p.ffn >> 5;           // blocks of the shared dimension

    // The slice of the shared dimension this workgroup answers.
    //
    // A product over few rows gives few workgroups — ffn_down at BM rows to a
    // group gives eighty, against sixty-four compute units that hold two each,
    // so the card is three fifths idle for the whole pass. Cutting the shared
    // dimension into slices multiplies the workgroups by the slice count and
    // costs one pass of shaders/matmul_reduce.comp over the answer.
    uint groups = (p.dim + uint(BM) - 1u) / uint(BM);
    uint ir  = gl_WorkGroupID.x % groups;
#ifdef BYID
    // The plan's entry this workgroup answers, and the expert's slab of the
    // stack the weights come out of.
    uint gi = gl_WorkGroupID.x / groups;
    if (gi >= plan[0]) return;
    uint e    = plan[1u + 2u * gi];
    uint col0 = plan[2u + 2u * gi];
    uint n    = counts[e];
    uint ik   = 0u;
#else
    uint ic  = (gl_WorkGroupID.x / groups) % uint(CGROUPS);
    uint ik  = gl_WorkGroupID.x / (groups * uint(CGROUPS));
    uint col0 = ic * uint(BN);
#endif
    uint per = ((nb + p.split - 1u) / p.split + uint(KSTEP) - 1u) & ~(uint(KSTEP) - 1u);
    uint kb0 = ik * per;
    uint kbn = min(nb, kb0 + per);
    uint row0 = ir * uint(BM);
    uint aw  = p.ffn >> 2;           // one column of the activation, in uints
    uint aw4 = aw >> 2;              // the same, in uvec4
    uint rowBytes = nb * 18u;
#ifdef BYID
    uint wbase = e * p.dim * rowBytes;   // this expert's slab of the stack
#else
    const uint wbase = 0u;
#endif
    // Sixteen-byte alignment of the nibbles, which is what lets the weights be
    // read as uvec4 as well.
    bool wide = (nb & 7u) == 0u;

    float acc[TM][TN];
    [[unroll]] for (uint r = 0u; r < TM; r++) {
        [[unroll]] for (uint c = 0u; c < TN; c++) acc[r][c] = 0.0;
    }

    // The staging, a step ahead of where it is used.
    //
    // Read and used in the same breath, the walk stalls on memory once a step:
    // there is nothing between issuing the loads and needing them but a
    // barrier. Measured against the fused gate kernel — which has no shared
    // memory and no barriers and streams its weights at three hundred and
    // nineteen gigabytes a second — this one was reaching ninety.
    //
    // So a step's loads are issued into registers, the compute of the previous
    // step runs, and only then are they written to shared memory. The card
    // waits for the loads at the write rather than at the barrier, with a
    // whole step of arithmetic in between to cover them.
    uvec4 wq[WLOADS];
    float wd[WLOADS];
    uvec4 av[ALOADS];
    float ad[ADLOADS];
    float ac[ADLOADS];

#define FETCH(kb) \
    [[unroll]] for (uint i = 0u; i < uint(WLOADS); i++) { \
        uint idx = t + i * uint(THREADS); \
        uint r = idx / KSTEP, k = idx & (KSTEP - 1u); \
        uint row = row0 + r, b = (kb) + k; \
        if (row < p.dim && b < nb) { \
            uint at = wbase + row * rowBytes + 2u * nb + 16u * b; \
            wq[i] = wide ? w4[at >> 4] \
                         : uvec4(w[(at >> 2)], w[(at >> 2) + 1u], w[(at >> 2) + 2u], w[(at >> 2) + 3u]); \
            wd[i] = unpackHalf2x16(w[(wbase + row * rowBytes + 2u * b) >> 2])[b & 1u]; \
        } else { \
            wq[i] = uvec4(0u); wd[i] = 0.0; \
        } \
    } \
    [[unroll]] for (uint i = 0u; i < uint(ALOADS); i++) { \
        uint idx = t + i * uint(THREADS); \
        uint rest = idx & (KSTEP * 2u - 1u); \
        uint c = idx / (KSTEP * 2u), k = rest >> 1, h = rest & 1u; \
        uint b = (kb) + k; \
        av[i] = (b < nb) ? aq4[ACOL(c) * aw4 + b * 2u + h] : uvec4(0u); \
    } \
    [[unroll]] for (uint i = 0u; i < uint(ADLOADS); i++) { \
        uint idx = t + i * uint(THREADS); \
        uint c = idx / KSTEP, k = idx & (KSTEP - 1u); \
        uint b = (kb) + k; \
        ad[i] = (b < nb) ? ASCALE(c, b) : 0.0; \
        ac[i] = (b < nb) ? ACORR(c, b) : 0.0; \
    }

#define STAGE() \
    [[unroll]] for (uint i = 0u; i < uint(WLOADS); i++) { \
        uint idx = t + i * uint(THREADS); \
        uint r = idx / KSTEP, k = idx & (KSTEP - 1u); \
        uint o = r * uint(SA_STRIDE) + k * 4u; \
        sa[o] = wq[i].x; sa[o + 1u] = wq[i].y; sa[o + 2u] = wq[i].z; sa[o + 3u] = wq[i].w; \
        sad[r * uint(SD_STRIDE) + k] = wd[i]; \
    } \
    [[unroll]] for (uint i = 0u; i < uint(ALOADS); i++) { \
        uint idx = t + i * uint(THREADS); \
        uint rest = idx & (KSTEP * 2u - 1u); \
        uint c = idx / (KSTEP * 2u), k = rest >> 1, h = rest & 1u; \
        uint o = c * uint(SB_STRIDE) + k * 8u + h * 4u; \
        sb[o] = av[i].x; sb[o + 1u] = av[i].y; sb[o + 2u] = av[i].z; sb[o + 3u] = av[i].w; \
    } \
    [[unroll]] for (uint i = 0u; i < uint(ADLOADS); i++) { \
        uint idx = t + i * uint(THREADS); \
        uint c = idx / KSTEP, k = idx & (KSTEP - 1u); \
        sbd[c * uint(SD_STRIDE) + k] = ad[i]; \
        sbc[c * uint(SD_STRIDE) + k] = ac[i]; \
    }

#ifdef BYID
    for (uint k = t; k < uint(BN); k += uint(THREADS)) {
        bool live  = col0 + k < n;
        spair[k]   = pairs[e * p.cap + (live ? col0 + k : col0)];
        salive[k]  = live;
    }
    barrier();
#endif

    FETCH(kb0)
    for (uint kb = kb0; kb < kbn; kb += uint(KSTEP)) {
        barrier();
        STAGE()
        barrier();
        if (kb + uint(KSTEP) < kbn) { FETCH(kb + uint(KSTEP)) }

        [[unroll]] for (uint k = 0u; k < KSTEP; k++) {
            // This thread's TM weight blocks, held while every column meets
            // them. That reuse is the whole point of the tile.
            uint  areg[TM][4];
            float ad[TM];
            [[unroll]] for (uint r = 0u; r < TM; r++) {
                uint row = tr * uint(TM) + r;
                ad[r] = sad[row * uint(SD_STRIDE) + k];
                [[unroll]] for (uint u = 0u; u < 4u; u++) {
                    areg[r][u] = sa[row * uint(SA_STRIDE) + k * 4u + u];
                }
            }

            [[unroll]] for (uint c = 0u; c < TN; c++) {
                uint col = tc * uint(TN) + c;
                uint breg[8];
                [[unroll]] for (uint u = 0u; u < 8u; u++) {
                    breg[u] = sb[col * uint(SB_STRIDE) + k * 8u + u];
                }
                float bd = sbd[col * uint(SD_STRIDE) + k], bc = sbc[col * uint(SD_STRIDE) + k];

                [[unroll]] for (uint r = 0u; r < TM; r++) {
                    int qsum = 0;
                    // Four products to an instruction. A Q4_0 word holds eight
                    // weights as nibbles, and a mask and a shift give four of
                    // them already sitting in the four bytes the dot product
                    // wants. The accumulator is integer and exact.
                    [[unroll]] for (uint u = 0u; u < 4u; u++) {
                        uint word = areg[r][u];
                        qsum = dotPacked4x8AccSatEXT(word & 0x0F0F0F0Fu,        int(breg[u]),      qsum);
                        qsum = dotPacked4x8AccSatEXT((word >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0Fu, int(breg[u + 4u]), qsum);
                    }
                    // The nibble is the weight plus eight, and the correction
                    // the activation carries is eight times its scale times
                    // the sum of its quanta. ggml folds the two here, once a
                    // block, rather than carrying a second accumulator.
                    acc[r][c] += ad[r] * (float(qsum) * bd - bc);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    barrier();
    [[unroll]] for (uint r = 0u; r < TM; r++) {
        uint row = row0 + tr * TM + r;
        if (row >= p.dim) continue;
        [[unroll]] for (uint c = 0u; c < TN; c++) {
#ifdef BYID
            uint col = tc * uint(TN) + c;
            if (!salive[col]) continue;
            y[spair[col] * p.dim + row] = acc[r][c];
#else
            y[(ik * COLUMNS + col0 + tc * TN + c) * p.dim + row] = acc[r][c];
#endif
        }
    }
}
